### 2.2 Central Trigger Barrel

The CTB is built from 240 plastic scintillating tiles set around the circumference of the TPC. The purpose of the CTB is to measure charged particle multiplicity. It is meant to be a fast (\(\sim\) 260 ns) detector operating within the 1.5 \(\mu\)s Level 0 trigger window allotted for the decision of whether or not to record an event.

The setup of the CTB is illustrated in Figure 2.17. The CTB barrel consists of 4 m long aluminum trays which each hold two radiators, two light guides, and two PMTs16. It provides full azimuthal coverage and pseudorapidity coverage of \(|\eta|<1\). When a collision occurs, charged particles interact electromagnetically with the scintillator tiles to produce light which is collected by a PMT. The number of charged particles crossing a given slat can then be deduced from the PMT signal.

