#### Coherent and Incoherent \(\rho^{0}\) Photoproduction in Ultra-Peripheral Au+Au Collisions

There are cross sections for both coherent and incoherent \(\rho^{0}\) production. When the transverse momentum of the photon is small, \(p_{T}<\hbar/R_{A}\), the subsequent \(q\overline{q}\) pair couples to the entire nucleus and the resulting \(\rho^{0}\) is coherently produced. At large transverse momentum, when the \(q\overline{q}\) couples to an individual nucleon, it is incoherently produced. Coherent vector meson production has a cross section equivalent to about 8-10% of the total hadronic cross section for Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}=200\) GeV [37, 46, 45, 69, 70].

Coherent and incoherent cross sections have been measured by STAR in Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}=200\) GeV, as shown in Figure 3.3. Table 3.1 gives the measured Figure 3.2. There are two photoproduction channels for the \(\rho^{0}\) in this study. In the diagram on the left, a virtual photon from the top gold nucleus fluctuates to a quark-antiquark pair and scatters off a Pomeron in the bottom nucleus to subsequently produce a \(\rho^{0}\). In the diagram on the right, the same process occurs as well as the exchange of additional photons which induce mutual Coulomb excitation and subsequent emission of neutrons from each nucleus. The exchange of the three (or more) photons actually occurs as three (or more) independent processes.

cross sections for coherent and incoherent \(\rho^{0}\) photoproduction with and without nuclear excitation.

#### Interference in \(\rho^{0}\) Photoproduction in Ultra-Peripheral Heavy Ion Collisions

In an effect akin to double-slit interference, there is interference in the \(\rho^{0}\) photoproduction cross section in ultraperipheral \(A\)+\(A\) collisions. Since either nucleus can be the source of the photon which scatters to produce a \(\rho^{0}\), the two possibilities for production are quantum-mechanically indistinguishable. Subsequently the wave func

